	<h3 class="h5">Hebrew dates expressed</h3>
	<p>
		A Gregorian day starts at midnight but a Hebrew day starts at dusk the evening before (~6 hours),
		therefore expressing a Hebrew day on a Gregorian calendar becomes complicated.
		This calendar resolved this by pairing each Hebrew day with an Icon + a Label (see <i>Yom Kippur</i> example below).
	</p>
	<div class="mt-1 mb-4 ms-3">
		<span class="@FeastGroupBadgeColor.YomKippur.Title mx-0"><i class="@FeastDay.YomKippur.Icon"></i></span>
		<span class="mx-0 px-0 text-black-50"><i class="fa-solid fa-grip-lines-vertical"></i></span>
		<span class="@FeastGroupBadgeColor.YomKippur.Title">@FeastDay.YomKippur.Title</span>
	</div>
	<p>
		The icon <span class="@FeastGroupBadgeColor.YomKippur.Title mx-0"><i class="@FeastDay.YomKippur.Icon"></i></span>
		represents the evening before (<b><i>erev</i></b>) and the label
		<span class="@FeastGroupBadgeColor.YomKippur.Title">@FeastDay.YomKippur.Title</span>
		represents the <b>day of</b>.
		You could say that the 
		<span class="mx-0 px-0 text-black-50"><i class="fa-solid fa-grip-lines-vertical"></i></span>
		represents mid-night
	</p>

	<h3 class="h5">The new moon (<i>Rosh Kodesh</i>) drives the other dates</h3>
	<p>
		Purim and Hanukkah follow rabbinic sources. Every other date is driven by the new-moon dates.
		Adding a year, or dates for another time zone, is the work required to extend the calendar.
	</p>

